WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.140
Freezing begins while the tone still plays.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.940
So why does the hormone peak only tens of minutes later?

3
00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:13.970
Because it runs through three relays: hypothalamus, pituitary, adrenal.

4
00:00:14.483 --> 00:00:20.063
Neurons in the hypothalamus release CRH into tiny vessels that lead to the

5
00:00:20.063 --> 00:00:21.063
pituitary.

6
00:00:21.283 --> 00:00:26.103
The pituitary answers by releasing ACTH into the general circulation.

7
00:00:26.833 --> 00:00:32.733
ACTH reaches the adrenal cortex, which makes cortisol, or corticosterone in rats.

8
00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:35.553
The pituitary answers within minutes.

9
00:00:36.293 --> 00:00:40.353
But the adrenal must make its hormone, so levels peak tens of minutes later.

10
00:00:40.983 --> 00:00:44.983
As the hormone rises, it feeds back and turns down further release.

11
00:00:45.863 --> 00:00:47.323
A low baseline carries on.

12
00:00:47.833 --> 00:00:49.633
This thermostat has two speeds.

13
00:00:50.473 --> 00:00:52.513
A fast arm acts over seconds to minutes.

14
00:00:53.117 --> 00:00:57.417
A slow arm works by changing which genes are read, over hours to days.

15
00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:01.230
Even with no threat at all, the hormone rises and falls.

16
00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:04.970
It has a daily rhythm, and pulses about once an hour.

17
00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:08.490
Is each hourly pulse a command from the hypothalamus?

18
00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:11.420
Walker and colleagues tested this in rats.

19
00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:15.520
They chose a time of day when the rat's own CRH is low.

20
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:19.560
So new pulses were unlikely to be the rat's own commands.

21
00:01:20.233 --> 00:01:23.293
Then they dripped in CRH at a perfectly steady rate.

22
00:01:24.093 --> 00:01:26.453
If every pulse is a command, what should you see?

23
00:01:32.017 --> 00:01:34.737
Commands alone would give a steady, raised level.

24
00:01:35.637 --> 00:01:38.877
Instead, the hormone still rose and fell, about once an hour.

25
00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:42.930
So in this preparation, pulsed CRH was not needed.

26
00:01:43.790 --> 00:01:48.090
Given a steady infusion, the pituitary and adrenal loop can make its own rhythm.

27
00:01:48.733 --> 00:01:50.433
Now, where does our rule stop?

28
00:01:51.293 --> 00:01:54.173
In rats, learning about a place also needed the hippocampus.

29
00:01:55.067 --> 00:01:59.707
A patient with selective damage to both amygdalae could still say which stimulus

30
00:01:59.707 --> 00:02:03.207
went with which. The learned bodily response was missing.

31
00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:08.370
And one breath of carbon dioxide made three women with that damage panic.

32
00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:11.430
That is three people, from one rare disease.

33
00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:15.640
Rat circuits can find necessary steps for a measured response.

34
00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.160
They cannot alone say what is felt.

35
00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:21.650
The amygdala is needed to learn an outside threat.

36
00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:27.230
Neural routes respond fast; the hormone route is slower, because the adrenal must

37
00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:27.890
make its hormone.
